          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Is I10 a complete code, or does it need a fourth character?",
          "answer": "**I10 is a complete code.** It has three characters and no fourth, fifth, or sixth character. The ICD-10-CM coding summary says I10 is \"unusual in being a complete code at three characters,\" so a request to add more detail to I10 cannot be met within the classification. (Source: *Coding hypertension: I10 and the hypertensive disease family*.)\n\n**The general convention:** ICD-10-CM codes run from three to seven characters. The three-character form is the category, and later characters add detail such as cause, site, severity, or laterality. The source says most categories need a fourth, fifth, or sixth character before the code is billable. I10 is the exception. (Source: *ICD-10-CM code structure and the conventions that govern selection*.)\n\nThe retrieved passages do not set out the full billing rules, such as payer requirements or which codes are accepted on claims. For those, check the current official ICD-10-CM tabular list and your payer's guidance.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
